[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Denormalization Strategies and Data Duplication in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cloud Firestore is a NoSQL, document-oriented database. Unlike relational databases that rely heavily on normalization and JOIN operations, Firestore is designed for read-heavy, deeply nested, or flattened document structures. Because Firestore does not support native server-side JOIN queries, denormalization and data duplication are essential architectural patterns for achieving high performance and low latency."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Firestore Performance Constraints"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Firestore, read operations scale with the number of documents returned, not the complexity of the query. Executing multiple queries to stitch together related data (client-side joins) increases latency, consumes more network bandwidth, and escalates billing costs. Denormalization solves this by duplicating necessary data across multiple documents so that a single document read provides all the required information."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Denormalization Patterns"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parent-Child Embedding: Storing small, bounded arrays or maps inside a parent document when the sub-data is strictly 1-to-1 or bounded 1-to-many."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference Duplication: Copying immutable or infrequently changing fields (such as user names and avatars) directly into related documents (like comments or posts)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Counters and Aggregates: Maintaining aggregate values (like total likes or comment counts) inside the parent document to avoid reading an entire subcollection."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Implementing Data Duplication"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a blogging application where posts have comments. Instead of querying a separate users collection every time to display a comment author's name and avatar, duplicate those fields inside the comment document."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"const commentData = {
  text: "Great article!",
  timestamp: admin.firestore.FieldValue.serverTimestamp(),
  author: {
    uid: "user_12345",
    displayName: "Jane Doe",
    avatarUrl: "https://example.com/avatar.jpg"
  }
};

await db.collection("posts").doc("post_abc").collection("comments").add(commentData);"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Data Updates with Cloud Functions"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The primary drawback of denormalization is data inconsistency. If Jane Doe updates her avatar, that change must propagate to every document where her data was duplicated. Firebase Cloud Functions are the ideal tool to automate this synchronization process."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"exports.onUserUpdate = functions.firestore
  .document("users/{userId}")
  .onUpdate(async (change, context) => {
    const newData = change.after.data();
    const userId = context.params.userId;

    const batch = db.batch();
    const commentsQuery = await db.collectionGroup("comments")
      .where("author.uid", "==", userId)
      .get();

    commentsQuery.forEach((doc) => {
      batch.update(doc.ref, {
        "author.displayName": newData.displayName,
        "author.avatarUrl": newData.avatarUrl
      });
    });

    return batch.commit();
  });"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Trade-offs"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prioritize Reads: Optimize for the user experience by prioritizing fast read operations over write complexity."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limit Duplication Scope: Only duplicate data that is read frequently and updated infrequently."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Atomic Batches: Always use Firestore Batches or Transactions when updating duplicated data to minimize partial update states."}]
